package wasdk:platform@2.2.0;

/// GPU memory the system's compositor can show: an `IOSurface`, an `AHardwareBuffer`, a shared D3D
/// texture; on the web, a `VideoFrame`. A program draws into one and samples it through a texture
/// it imports on its own GPU device (`gpu-interop`), shows it on a composition layer (`views`'
/// `composition-layer.present`), and has the system's codecs encode it
/// (`wasdk:media/image-codecs`). A buffer holds only its own facts, its size and format and what
/// it can be used for, and names no GPU API: the GPU side imports it, as each system's GPU API
/// imports such memory, while the CPU reads it back here, as the buffer's own lock does
/// (`IOSurfaceLock`, `AHardwareBuffer_lock`).
///
/// **Raw memory and lent frames.** A hardware buffer is memory the program holds: one it allocates
/// (`allocate`), as `IOSurfaceCreate` and `AHardwareBuffer_allocate` make one, and an image the
/// system's hardware codecs decode, as Image I/O decodes into an `IOSurface` and Android's
/// `ImageDecoder` into a hardware bitmap; no system pool waits for it. A video's frame is apart: a
/// decoder's or a camera's, lent with its slot in the system's pool (`wasdk:media/video-frames`),
/// whose `hardware-buffer` is one of these that keeps the frame, and its slot, while it is held.
///
/// **Two kinds of memory.** A hardware buffer is GPU memory, drawn with no copy; a pixel buffer is
/// the program's own memory, which it uploads with WebGPU itself to draw. Every move between them
/// is a call of its own, so a program sees each copy it makes: a hardware buffer comes back to the
/// program's memory only through `read-pixels`.
///
/// **How much.** Only the system bounds what a program holds of the hardware buffers it allocates
/// and decodes, as only the GPU bounds a WebGPU device's allocations: where the system has no
/// memory left, an allocation or a decode answers `out-of-memory`. A buffer the program lets go
/// of, once every texture imported of it and every layer showing it have let go too, gives its
/// memory back.
interface hardware-buffers {
  /// The system's hardware buffer allocator; none where the system allocates no hardware buffers.
  get-hardware-buffer-allocator: func() -> option<hardware-buffer-allocator>;

  /// What makes a program's hardware buffers: the role of Android's graphics allocator
  /// (gralloc's `IAllocator`), Fuchsia's sysmem `Allocator` and a GBM device on Linux.
  resource hardware-buffer-allocator {
    /// A buffer of `width` by `height` pixels in `format`, for at least what `usage` names (the
    /// buffer's `usage` answers all it is for), its pixels as the system leaves new memory until
    /// the program draws them.
    /// `unsupported` for a format the system does not allocate a buffer in: Apple's systems
    /// allocate `bgra8`, Android's `AHardwareBuffer` `rgba8`, Windows' shared textures and the web
    /// both. `too-large` for a side past the largest the system makes a texture of, 16384 on
    /// Apple's systems. `out-of-memory` where the system has no memory left for it. A side of no
    /// pixels is the program's mistake, and traps.
    allocate: func(width: u32, height: u32, format: pixel-format, usage: hardware-buffer-usage) -> result<hardware-buffer, image-error>;

    /// Whether `allocate` makes a buffer in `format` for `usage` on this system: what it would
    /// answer `unsupported` to, asked first. The format to draw in for the screen, with no
    /// conversion, is WebGPU's preferred canvas format.
    @since(version = 2.2.0)
    supports: func(format: pixel-format, usage: hardware-buffer-usage) -> bool;
  }

  /// Pixels that the GPU and the system's compositor both reach: an `IOSurface`, an
  /// `AHardwareBuffer`; on the web, a `VideoFrame`. A video frame's buffer keeps the frame, and
  /// its slot in the decoder's pool, while it and every texture imported of it are held.
  resource hardware-buffer {
    width: func() -> u32;
    height: func() -> u32;
    /// What the buffer can be used for: at least what it was allocated for. A texture imported of
    /// it allows what this allows, and `read-pixels` reads it only where this has `cpu-read`.
    usage: func() -> hardware-buffer-usage;
    /// The buffer's pixels copied into the program's memory, in the format the buffer holds them,
    /// once the GPU has finished the work the program submitted and the writes it queued before
    /// this call: BGRA on Apple's systems, RGBA on Android. `unsupported` for a buffer whose
    /// `usage` lacks `cpu-read`, on every system, and for one whose pixels are in no
    /// `pixel-format`.
    read-pixels: async func() -> result<pixel-buffer, image-error>;
  }

  /// What a buffer can be used for, named after its users, as Android's `AHardwareBuffer` usage
  /// is: what the GPU does with it, which a texture imported of it allows (`gpu-interop`), and
  /// whether the system's compositor and the CPU reach it. A video encoder joins as it is
  /// needed.
  ///
  /// The usage a program allocates a buffer for is the least the buffer is for, and the buffer
  /// answers all it is for (`hardware-buffer.usage`), which every check reads. Android gives a
  /// buffer what it was allocated for, as `AHardwareBuffer_describe` answers. Apple's systems
  /// give a buffer the program allocates every use, whatever it asked, since any `IOSurface` can
  /// be sampled, drawn into, shown by Core Animation and read by the CPU; a decoded image's buffer
  /// and a video frame's are `gpu-sampled`, as the system made them, with `composer-overlay` and
  /// `cpu-read`, which any `IOSurface` has. A program that uses a buffer for more than it asked
  /// works on Apple's systems and is refused on Android, and the engine says so in its log.
  flags hardware-buffer-usage {
    /// The GPU samples it in its shaders: Android's `GPU_SAMPLED_IMAGE`.
    gpu-sampled,
    /// The GPU draws into it, as a render pass's colour output, and copies into it: Android's
    /// `GPU_COLOR_OUTPUT`.
    gpu-color-output,
    /// The system's compositor may show it without the GPU, as a display's overlay, when a
    /// composition layer presents it: Android's `COMPOSER_OVERLAY`. Without it Android composites
    /// the layer on the GPU, which shows the same and costs power. Android composites on the GPU
    /// wherever it shows no overlay, as an emulator always does, and blacks out a buffer the GPU
    /// may not sample there: a buffer a layer shows is `gpu-sampled` too.
    composer-overlay,
    /// The CPU reads it, through `read-pixels`, which answers `unsupported` for a buffer without
    /// it: Android's `CPU_READ_RARELY` or `CPU_READ_OFTEN`, since Android locks a buffer only for
    /// the CPU use it was allocated with. Allocating asks for `CPU_READ_RARELY`.
    cpu-read,
  }

  /// Pixels in the program's memory: `height` rows from the top, each `row-bytes` long and
  /// starting with `width` pixels of four bytes, in `format`, their colour in sRGB. A row may end
  /// in padding, as a system lays rows out for its own copies.
  record pixel-buffer {
    width: u32,
    height: u32,
    /// The bytes from one row's start to the next's: at least four for each pixel.
    row-bytes: u32,
    format: pixel-format,
    alpha: alpha-type,
    /// `height` rows of `row-bytes`, exactly.
    bytes: list<u8>,
  }

  /// The order of a pixel's four channels, each a byte.
  enum pixel-format {
    /// Red, green, blue, alpha: Android's `ARGB_8888` as it lies in memory, the web's
    /// `ImageData`.
    rgba8,
    /// Blue, green, red, alpha: Apple's 32-bit BGRA, Windows' `Bgra8`.
    bgra8,
  }

  /// How a pixel's colour relates to its alpha: Android's premultiplied bitmaps, Windows'
  /// `BitmapAlphaMode`, Apple's alpha info.
  enum alpha-type {
    /// Its colour is multiplied by its alpha already, as GPUs and compositors blend.
    premultiplied,
    /// Its colour is apart from its alpha, as PNG stores it.
    straight,
  }

  /// Why pixels were not read, decoded or encoded, or a buffer not allocated.
  record image-error {
    kind: image-error-kind,
    /// The system's own words, for logs: not for showing as they are.
    message: string,
  }

  /// The kind of a failure, which a program words for its user and acts on.
  enum image-error-kind {
    /// A format the system does not decode, encode or allocate, or pixels in a layout it does not
    /// read.
    unsupported,
    /// The bytes are of a format the system reads, and are not a whole image of it.
    damaged,
    /// The image is larger than the system decodes it at into that memory, asked for at a
    /// `max-size` it may decode; or a buffer larger than the system allocates.
    too-large,
    /// No file at the path among the program's files.
    not-found,
    /// More memory than the system has left.
    out-of-memory,
    /// Anything else the system reports.
    other,
  }
}
